  "query_preview": "..\n\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Anticipate cooler temperatures and periods of valley rain and \n   mountain snow this week.\n\nA low pressure system is moving onshore over Washington today. This system\nwill move east, bringing widespread rain and snow to the Northern Rockies\nlater today and tonight. Snow levels tonight are expected to lower to\naround 4,000 to 5,000 feet, bringing accumulating snow to the mountains.\nThe heaviest snow is expected to fall across the Mission Mountains up into\nGlacier National Park, where snowfall amounts have increased\ncompared to recent days. Several inches of snow can be expected in\nthese areas above 5000ft by Tuesday afternoon. Those with \nbackcountry plans through the overnight, need to be prepared for \nwintry conditions. The valleys across northwest Montana, including\nKalispell, may see periods of snow mixing with the rain, though \nthe accumulating snow will remain in the mountains.\n\nAnother weather system will bring more rain and mountain snow to the area\nWednesday through Thursday. A cooler Canadian air mass will move south and\nwest over the Continental Divide by Thursday morning, which could lower\nsnow levels down to 3,000 feet for locations along Highway 2. If we see any\nimpacts in the valleys due to snow, Thursday morning would be the day,\nwhere around one-half to 1 inch of snow could accumulate before\nsunrise.\n\nBy Friday, warmer air will move into the region ahead of another storm\nsystem, causing snow levels to rise. This storm system will move through\nover the weekend, lowering snow levels once again and bringing another\nround of widespread rain and snow.",
